<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Finance</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Remember that if you invest <m>P</m> dollars at an annual interest rate <m>r</m>
compounded continuously, then after <m>T</m> years, you will have
<md>
Pe^{rT}\hbox{ dollars.}
</md></p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that you invest $1000 at 7%.  How much will you have
  after 10 years?</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>After you successfully finish Math 202, you get a job computing
  volumes all day, and you are able to save $10,000 a year (this is
  called an <em>income stream</em>).</p>
        <p>Suppose that you will retire in 30 years, continually saving
  $10,000 a year and investing it in an account at an annual interest
  rate of 7%.  We would like to determine how much money you will
  have at the end of 30 years.</p>
        <p>Shown below is a representation of the 30-year interval during which
  you will be working.  Time <m>t</m> represents the amount of time since you
  start working.</p>
        <p><image source="figures/timeline.eps"/></p>
        <p>In the timeline above, a time interval of length <m>dt</m> is shown.  How
  much money do you save during this time interval of length <m>dt</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>If you deposit this money <m>t</m> years after you start working, how
  long is in the account before your retirement?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>How much is it worth when you retire?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the total amount of money that you have when you retire?  This
  is called the <em>future value</em> of the income stream.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>On the day you begin working, you learn that you've won the lottery.
  Your first thought is to quit work and invest the winnings at 7%.
  How much would you need to have won so that, after 30 years, you have
  the same amount you would have had if you worked and saved?  This is
  called the <em>present value</em> of the income stream.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>Let's imagine a different scenario: the longer you work, the
  more money you make and hence the more money you save.  For instance,
  suppose that you save at a rate of <m>P(t) = 10,000+1000t</m> dollars per
  year.  How much will you have when you retire after 30 years?</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>If you would like to retire after 25 years with one million
  dollars, at what constant rate should you save money?</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that you save $5000 per year and
  you want to retire with one million dollars.  How long do you need
  to work?</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that in your work for an oil company, you discover an
  oil field.  Initially, it is easy to take oil from the field, but it
  becomes more difficult over time.  Therefore, the rate at which you
  can extract oil is
  <md>
  r(t) = 10 - 0.5t
  </md>
  millions of barrels per year.</p>
        <p>How long does it take to remove all the oil?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>How much oil is there in the field?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Assuming that the price of oil stays constant at $50 per barrel and
  an annual investment interest rate of 8%, what is the future value
  of the oil field?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose you want to sell the oil field.  Why would you want to know
  the present value of the field?  What is the present value?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
